{
  "at": "",
  "command": "change",
  "counts": {
    "errors": 0,
    "skipped": 0,
    "stale_baseline": 0,
    "suppressed": {
      "baseline": 0,
      "ignored": 0,
      "labeled_fp": 0
    }
  },
  "docs": {
    "architecture": "result.architecture on history: how the inferred import graph changed over the window. periods has one row per week with a commit, read from the tree after the week's last first-parent commit (rev). A commit authored before the commits it follows counts in their week, so the rows run in walk order. cycles and largest_cycle are counted as import_cycles counts them, and files_in_cycles, depth_dirs, and median_depth have their own entries. A week that changed no source file and no go.mod repeats the week before. Each tree is resolved from cached fingerprints, so the first run parses the files the window never touched and later runs parse nothing. The text table prints the first week, the last, and each week whose cycle counts changed. Nothing here is a finding: there is no rule id, no baseline, and no gate. See depth_moved, cycle_origin, and hidden_coupling for the three lists beside periods.",
    "blocks": "Per group in result.jsx_groups: how many separate blocks of markup the group's units share, site for site. The sites give the lines of the one that ranks highest alone, and elements and holes count them all.",
    "case_sets": "result.case_sets on snapshot with --experimental: sets of cases the tree enumerates in more than one place, with total and the 20 families that rank highest. A place is a switch, a type switch, or a match of 3 arms or more, an if chain of 3 branches or more that test one subject against constants, or a literal table of 4 entries or more: a Go map, array, or slice written with keys, a Python dictionary, a TypeScript object. A case is a string, a number, a name, the last two parts of a dotted name (Color.RED, token.ADD), or a type in a type switch, an isinstance or instanceof test, or a class pattern. True, false, the null of the language, a number under 10, a string under 2 characters, and a type every program tests for (str, int, string, error, Array) are no case, and a place with fewer than 3 cases left is not read. A table counts when four fifths of its values are functions (a name, a member of something, a lambda or arrow function, a partial) or four fifths of its keys are named constants. It does not count when two fifths of its entries copy a field of the key's name off a value (name: user.name), which is a record, when it is written as an argument of a call, which is the call's named arguments, or when it is a TypeScript object of methods, which implements an interface. Two places are joined when they share 3 cases that at most 40 places of the tree hold, and a third of the smaller one's cases. The place with the heaviest joins and every place joined to it are a family, then the same over what is left. A place that names under 3 of the cases its family settles on is left for a later family, and no place is in two. Per family: cases (the cases at least half the sites name, and at least two of them), sites, weight, and rank. Per site: path, unit, kind (switch, if, or table), line and end_line, cases (how many cases the place names, the family's or its own), default (an arm takes every other case: an else, a default, a wildcard), and missing (the family's cases the place does not name). A missing case at a site with no default is one the place may have forgotten, and one at a site with a default, or at a table read with a fallback, may be meant. Left out: test files and test directories, the stand-alone directories and story files x/new-duplicate leaves out, and directories named scripts, migrations, alembic, seed, seeds, seed_data, seeders, or fixture. Not seen: a case set more than 40 places enumerate, a set of fewer than 3 cases, a Go literal of a named map type, and a subject compared through a helper. Gated because one person read every family and two of the guards were chosen on the repositories first read: of the 64 families listed in 22 public repositories 46 were a set a maintainer would tie together or want to know of, 16 a judgment call, and 2 not. No target. A family says a new case needs one edit per site: a table or a type the sites read would need one.",
    "churn_14d": "Lines added in the range that were modified or deleted within 14 days of landing. High churn reads as work that did not settle.",
    "churn_30d": "Lines added in the range that were modified or deleted within 30 days.",
    "concentration": "Largest single author's share of each directory's live lines. Reported per directory, never as a person ranking. A directory with no live lines reads as a dash in text and null in JSON.",
    "confidence": "Per pair in architecture.hidden_coupling: co_changes over the smaller of changes_a and changes_b. At 1 the file that changes less never changed without the other.",
    "counts": "Files the run could not score: skipped (excluded by config, size, binary content, minified or generated shape) and errors (a parse that left error nodes). Metrics exclude them, so a non-zero count means the numbers cover less of the tree. Unsupported file types are listed as skipped but not counted. suppressed counts the findings set aside; see suppressed. stale_baseline counts baseline entries that match nothing; see stale_baseline.",
    "coupling": "File pairs changed in the same commit 3+ times, skipping commits over 30 files (code-maat's definition of co-change).",
    "cycle_origin": "architecture.cycles: each import cycle of the last week, with id and members as its x/import-cycle finding has them and size counting every file. A cycle continues an earlier one when the two share at least two files, after following renames: two files that import each other in both weeks are one dependency that held, which includes the grown and the shrunk cycle a baseline entry covers. first_week and first_size are the week and size at which that line of cycles began, and a cycle that went away and came back begins again. introduced is the first-parent commit the cycle is first present after, found by bisecting that week's commits over cached fingerprints, with the commit's author class and intent. It is exact unless the cycle came and went inside the week, where it names one of the commits that brought it back, and a merge commit stands for its whole branch. before_window is set, with introduced null, when the cycle is already in the tree before the window's first commit, or when a shallow clone cannot read that tree. The author class is the configured class of the commit and never a person.",
    "deaths_30d": "How the lines counted in churn_30d died, per directory (credited to the directory the line was written in) and overall, as counts: total, self, pure, whole_file, in_revert, three age buckets (age_le_1d, age_2_7d, age_8_30d), and by_class (birth author class, then the class of the removing commit). Each split is a count of total, so a consumer can add directories. Moved lines are not deaths. Squash merges hide whatever happened inside a branch, so a repository that squashes shows only the survivors of each branch. Read the parts side by side and never as one score: pruning and flapping look alike in churn_30d and differ here.",
    "depth": "Per directory on snapshot: hidden_mass / (shown_mass + hidden_mass), or by name count, hidden / (shown + hidden), when neither side has a function to weigh (depth_by_mass false, # in the table). After Ousterhout's deep modules: near 1 the directory hides much more than callers must learn, near 0 callers see nearly all of it. A ratio with no target and no gate. It is null (- in the table) when shown is 0: nothing outside uses the directory, so it is an entry point or a leaf, not a module with everything hidden. depth counts methods matched by name as shown, which makes it a lower bound, and depth_attributed is the upper one; the table prints both as a range when they differ. depth_provisional (*) marks fewer than 5 names. The table lists shallowest first and leaves the masses to the JSON. When a directory you expected to hide its workings reads shallow, look at widest_surface and ask which of those names callers need.",
    "depth_attributed": "Per directory on snapshot: depth with every method matched by name counted as hidden, so only uses a resolved import attributes count as shown. No method can be attributed, so this is the upper bound and depth the lower: uses the tool cannot see aside, the true ratio lies between them. It is null when no use is attributed. A narrow range means the number is solid, and a wide one means the directory is mostly classes whose methods the tool cannot follow. On directories checked by hand, depth was the closer of the two wherever methods carry the mass.",
    "depth_dirs": "Per week in architecture.periods: the directories that other directories use names of and that declare at least 5 names, which are the ones median_depth is taken over. A directory under 5 names has a provisional depth and is left out.",
    "depth_moved": "architecture.depth_moved: up to 5 directories whose module depth moved most between the first and the last week of the window. A directory is listed when it has a depth off at least 5 names in both weeks and both depth and depth_attributed moved the same way by 0.05 or more. moved is the smaller of the two moves, so a method matched by name on one side alone cannot make a row. A directory that only one of the two weeks has is not listed. A fall means other directories came to use more of what the directory holds.",
    "drop": "split.drop in result.split_candidates on snapshot: a function's cognitive complexity less the larger of what the split leaves in it and what the moved block scores alone. See split_candidates.",
    "dup_literal": "x/dup-literal, on change with --experimental: a string the change wrote out in a new place that the tree already writes out in other files. It is the pass behind result.literal_groups (see literal_groups) over the tree at the end of the change: a group is reported when the change added one of its places and at least one other was there before. A place is new when the change added its line, the file writes the string more often than it did, and so do the changed files taken together, so a place that moved to another file or was edited on its line is not. The declaration of the constant that names the string is never the new place: adding it is the fix. The fingerprint keeps the lines of the first 4 places of a string per file, so a fifth place added to one file is counted and not located, and is not reported. The finding sits on the new place, its match is the value, clipped to 60 bytes, and its note gives the places and files that write it, up to three of the files, and either the constant that names it, with where it is declared, or how many places test it. A string the change writes in several new places is reported once, on the first. --brief prints the note as the reason of the next line. Not reported: a group whose every place the change added, and whatever literal_groups leaves out. Before schema 12 this rule reported on snapshot, once per file per string with no filter, which ran to megabytes on a large tree; a baseline entry from then matches nothing, is not counted in stale_baseline, which counts the rules a snapshot runs, and goes when the baseline is rewritten. Only change reports it, so label cannot find its id. When a hit is right, read the constant, or declare one and read it at each place. When the place should spell the value out, say why in a slop-strata:ignore comment on the finding's line.",
    "duplicate_groups": "result.duplicate_groups on snapshot with --experimental: code the tree holds more than once, with total, the removable tokens summed over every group, and the 20 groups with the most. It is the pass behind x/new-duplicate (see new_duplicate) with every statement of the tree read as new. Two stretches of statements pair when they line up statement for statement once identifiers and literals are set aside, and the pair's score, its tokens weighted by how rare each statement's shape is less 8 per name the two differ in, reaches 20. The heaviest pair becomes a group, and every other stretch of the tree with the same statements joins it, fewest differences first, while the group still meets that floor and its sites differ in at most 3 names together. No statement is in two groups. Then an expression of 20 tokens or more that repeats in statements no group holds becomes a group of its own (expression is true and statements 0), each site in a different function. Per group: sites (path, unit, the line it starts on, and last_line, where its last statement starts), statements and tokens of one site with signatures left out, parameters (per name the sites spell differently, each site's text in the order of sites, which is what a shared function would take), and removable. Left out as for x/new-duplicate: tests and directories of stand-alone files, two files of one name that are 80% alike, a stretch right behind its copy in one function, shapes the tree holds in 32 other places, and generated files; and also a group that is a table of values, where under a tenth of the identifiers and literals are identifiers. Gated because of what it still lists: of the top 5 groups of twelve public repositories, read by one person, 32 were worth merging, 16 a judgment call, and 12 not (all 20 Go groups held up, 15 of 20 Python, 13 of 20 TypeScript). The 12 were one file per locale, package, or API flavor that is kept parallel on purpose, declarations a TypedDict and its class both need, and public wrappers whose body is one forwarding call. No target. Start from the top: the list is where a merge would remove the most.",
    "elements": "Per group in result.jsx_groups: the elements one site holds, over every block of the group. An element is one tag with its attributes; a fragment and a part of a drawing count none, and an svg counts one.",
    "erosion": "Share of complexity mass (CC x sqrt(SLOC) per function) held by functions with cyclomatic complexity over 10, per arXiv 2603.24755. No target; watch direction per directory. When it rises on routine work, the complex functions are getting more of them.",
    "errors": "Files the parser could not fully read, with the byte share under error nodes. Metrics still count what parsed; the row is a warning, not an abort.",
    "experimental": "Rules not yet shown to clear 80% precision on the corpus, among them the narrating-comment, dup-literal, dup-test-case, dead-export, import-cycle, new-duplicate, new-case-set, new-jsx-shape, new-sibling, and snapshot stale-prose rules and the type-any and test-reads-source families. The same switch turns on a snapshot's split_candidates, duplicate_groups, case_sets, jsx_groups, sibling_sets and literal_groups lists, which are gated for the same reason. Enable them per run with --experimental, or for a repository with experimental = true in .slop-strata.toml. The owner decides when a rule leaves this list. Verdicts recorded with slop-strata label give each rule a measured precision for this repository, which slop-strata rules prints and the table below repeats from the labels file of the current directory. Labels never promote or demote a rule on their own, and one repository's labels are not the corpus the 80% bar is set on.",
    "fan_in": "Per directory on snapshot: the distinct other directories with a file that imports one of this directory's files, over resolved edges of every kind (see import_edges). A production row counts edges between production files, and a test row counts edges that start or end at a test file. High fan_in means many places break when this directory changes: keep its surface small and stable. Imports the tool cannot resolve are not counted, so a directory reached only through a path alias reads low.",
    "fan_out": "Per directory on snapshot: the distinct other directories its files import, on the same basis as fan_in. High fan_out means the directory knows about much of the tree. A directory high on both is a hub: a change there is felt everywhere and it is hard to test alone, so look there first when you split responsibilities.",
    "files_in_cycles": "Per week in architecture.periods: how many files sit in any import cycle, the sum of the sizes of the week's cycles. A count that rises while cycles stays flat means a cycle is growing.",
    "findings": "New rule hits attributable to this change, after the baseline, ignored, and labeled hits and the pre-existing hits are excluded. Each has an id, a short hash of the rule, path, enclosing function, matched text, and line text, so it survives line moves and new hits above it, and a scope naming the function. Editing the line changes the id. An x/import-cycle finding is keyed by its member files instead; see import_cycles. To suppress a hit inline, write slop-strata:ignore RULE-ID REASON in a comment on its line or the line above, in the file's comment syntax: // or # in code, # in TOML and YAML, and an HTML comment in Markdown outside code blocks. In Go, //nolint:errcheck // REASON also suppresses go/silent-error. The reason is required. An ignore without one suppresses nothing and is reported as x/ignore-without-reason. JSON files and ast-grep findings take no inline ignore. Suppressed hits are counted in counts.suppressed. To dispute a hit without touching the code, run slop-strata label ID fp. The exceptions are x/last-ref, x/new-duplicate, x/new-case-set, x/new-jsx-shape, and x/new-sibling, which only change reports: label cannot find their ids, so accept one with a baseline or labels file entry for the id. See new_duplicate, new_case_set, new_jsx_shape, and new_sibling.",
    "forwarders": "Per directory on snapshot, in the JSON: functions and methods whose body is one statement, a call whose every argument is one of the function's own parameters (a docstring and comments aside). A forwarder counts in neither shown nor hidden, so pass-through wrappers cannot pad a directory into looking deep. When another directory uses a forwarder, the name it forwards to is shown in its place if the same directory declares it: a function by name, or a method of the same receiver.",
    "hidden": "Per directory on snapshot: the declared names (see shown) that no production file of another directory uses, whether the directory uses them itself or nothing does. Pure forwarders are left out (see forwarders).",
    "hidden_coupling": "architecture.hidden_coupling: up to 10 pairs of production source files that change together though no chain of imports leads from either to the other in the last week's graph, over imports of every kind. co_changes counts the commits that changed both files, less the ones left out below, changes_a and changes_b count the commits that changed each, and support and confidence have their own entries. A pair needs 4 co_changes, a support of 1.5, and a confidence of 0.5. Left out of the counting: commits that touch more than 30 files, bot commits, docs and chore commits (ci, build, style, and dependency bumps read as chore), test files, files the engine does not score (generated, minified, oversized, unsupported), and any commit that also changed a file both files import, since that import explains the change. Left out as pairs: two Go files of one directory, which are one package, and two TypeScript files under different package.json files, which may import each other by a package name the graph does not resolve. shared_importer says some file imports both. A file keeps its counts through a rename. On twelve public repositories 7 pairs cleared these floors and all 7 read as real when checked by hand, against 20 of 30 at looser floors, so expect few rows. A pair listed here is a rule the code does not state: name it in a shared module or a test, or accept it.",
    "hidden_mass": "Per directory on snapshot: the complexity mass behind the hidden names, plus the mass of functions no declaration holds, such as a callback passed at file scope. Ten shown one-liners over a large hidden engine read deep by mass and shallow by count, which is why depth uses mass.",
    "holes": "Per group in result.jsx_groups: how many of the attribute values, runs of text, and wrapping expressions of the group's blocks are not the same at every site, which is what shared components would take as props. Values are compared by a 16-bit hash with white space left out, so one in 65,536 differing values reads as the same. A group with 0 holes is the same markup at every site.",
    "hotspot": "Where recent edits meet complexity mass. On snapshot, for each directory and file, touches_90d times mass: the commits that touched it in the 90 days ending at the scored revision's commit date, times its production mass (table_mass left out). Tests rank apart, and ties rank by mass. On history, 30-day churned lines times live mass per directory. Read the top rows first when you choose what to simplify, and a directory still being built will score high.",
    "import_cycles": "result.imports.cycles on snapshot: groups of two or more non-test files where each imports every other, directly or through the others, over module-level runtime imports. largest_cycle is the file count of the biggest group. Imports a type checker alone reads, imports inside a function body, and imports under a main guard are the usual ways around a cycle and do not count, and neither does a cycle that needs a test file to close. Go forbids package cycles, so Go files never form one. The numbers are counted before the baseline. With --experimental each group is one x/import-cycle finding on the import line of its cheapest cut, the edge importing the fewest names, with the shortest cycle through that edge in the note. The finding's id comes from the rule and the sorted member files (members), leaving out files that only re-export when two others remain, so it holds while imports move and changes when a file joins or leaves. A baseline entry for a cycle also covers any cycle made only of its members, so shrinking a cycle is never a new finding, and a new or grown cycle is. change reports a cycle the change created or grew. Move the names the cheapest cut imports into a module both sides can import.",
    "import_edges": "In result.imports on snapshot: edges counts every import the tool resolved from one file of the tree to another, type_edges the ones only a type checker reads (TypeScript import type, Python under TYPE_CHECKING, any import of or from a .pyi or .d.ts file), and deferred_edges the ones that do not run when the file loads (inside a function body, a dynamic import(), or under Python's if __name__ == \"__main__\"). The rest run at module level. Python resolves absolute and relative imports under any source root and drops a module name that two roots answer to. TypeScript resolves relative specifiers only, so path aliases and workspace package names add no edge. Go resolves through the module path of each go.mod. Imports of installed packages are outside the graph. No target; the numbers say how much of the tree fan_in, fan_out, and import_cycles rest on.",
    "in_revert": "Young deaths in commits that revert another change. A revert is neither pruning nor flapping by these measures, so it is counted apart and still included in total.",
    "intent": "The change's stated purpose read off commit metadata: Conventional Commit type, branch name, then issue keywords, plus revert detection. Kinds are feat, fix, refactor, perf, test, docs, chore, and unknown. Only refactor, fix, and test make a claim the numbers can contradict, so a reading that says \"reads as\" questions that claim. perf, docs, and chore never do. Use it to judge whether a mismatch between label and numbers is worth a question in review.",
    "jsx_groups": "result.jsx_groups on snapshot with --experimental: blocks of markup the tree holds more than once under other text and other attribute values, with total and the 20 groups that rank highest. Markup is the JSX of .tsx and .jsx files. A block is an element that holds 6 elements or more, itself counted. Two blocks have one shape when they have the same tags as written, the same attribute names whatever their order, and the same children in the same order. What a shared component would take as a prop is a hole and no part of the shape: the value of an attribute, a run of text and expressions between two elements, and the code around markup that an expression wraps, such as the condition of an \u0026\u0026 or the list a .map runs over. The kind of that expression is part of the shape, so a block under a condition and one under a loop are two shapes. An svg element is one element and one hole whatever it draws, and the parts of a drawing (g, path, circle, rect, and the like) count as no element, so two icons are no group. The blocks of one shape are a group when its sites differ in at most one hole per element: past that they share a skeleton a component library dictates and little of what they say. A block inside a block of a group with as many sites is not listed again. The blocks that repeat across the same units, site for site, are one group, since two forms that share five fields are one thing to merge and not five. Per group: sites, blocks, elements, holes, and rank. Per site: path, unit (the smallest declaration that holds it), and line and end_line of the block, the largest one for a group of several. Left out: test files and test directories, and the stand-alone directories and story files x/new-duplicate leaves out. Not seen: a block of under 6 elements, two blocks that differ in one tag or one attribute name, markup built without JSX, and repeated logic with no markup, which duplicate_groups reads. Gated because one person read the groups and both guards (the drawing rule, and one group per set of units) were chosen on repositories read first: of 51 groups read among the 20 listed in each of five public React applications 37 were a block a maintainer would make one component of, 14 a judgment call, and none wrong, and of the 20 in the two applications no guard was fitted to, 13 and 7. Lower in a long list more are judgment calls: see DESIGN.md. No target. A group says the sites are one component written out several times: a change to the block needs an edit per site.",
    "labels": "Verdicts recorded with slop-strata label ID tp or fp, in the labels file (default .slop-strata-labels.json, set by labels in .slop-strata.toml). A finding labeled fp is suppressed and counted in counts.suppressed.labeled_fp, and one labeled tp stays. Each entry holds the rule, verdict, path, matched text, note, and the tool version, and no author. The file is evidence of this repository's precision per rule. It says nothing about a rule's precision on other repositories, which the 80% gate measures. Label hits as you review them, then read slop-strata rules.",
    "literal_groups": "result.literal_groups on snapshot with --experimental: strings the tree writes out in 3 files and 4 places or more where one name would do, with total and the 20 groups that rank highest. A string is the content of a string literal of 8 to 200 bytes on one line, whatever quotes it is written in, with no value written into it and no % in it. A place is one such literal in the code. No place: a module path, the key of a mapping, a docstring or a directive, a part of a string written in pieces, a Go struct tag, an attribute of JSX markup, and the operand of a typeof test. A place is a use when the code branches or looks something up on the string: an operand of ==, !=, in, or not in, a member of the list such a test reads or that includes is called on, a case of a switch or a match, the key of a subscript, or the first argument of a call to get, has, set, pop, setdefault, delete, getItem, setItem, removeItem, getenv, Getenv, LookupEnv, Lookup, getAttribute, setAttribute, or hasAttribute. A constant names the string when the string is the whole value of a Go const or var of one name at file scope, a Python assignment to one name at module or class level, a TypeScript const at file scope, or a TypeScript enum member; that declaration is one of the places. A string is listed in two cases. A constant names it and 2 places or more write it out all the same. Or 2 places or more are uses and at least one place is not, so the program both produces the value and tests it. Left out in both cases: a string some file writes as a type (a TypeScript literal type, a member of an array or object under as const, an argument of a schema's enum or literal call, a Python annotation, a member of a Python Literal), since the type checker holds its spelling; a string that is also an identifier somewhere in the tree, which is the name of a field, a key, or a member and not a value; a Python dunder name; and a string whose every file has one base name (a route.ts per directory), which are kept parallel on purpose. Per group: value, constant (name, path, line, when one names it), files, written, uses, rank, and sites. Per site, one per file in path order and at most 8 of them: path, line (the first place in the file), written, and uses. Left out: test files and test directories, the stand-alone directories and story files x/new-duplicate leaves out, the directories case_sets leaves out (scripts, migrations, seeds), and a tool's configuration file (*.config.*). Not seen: a string under 8 bytes, a string built from parts, a value compared through a helper, a number, and what a type outside the tree checks, such as a status a vendor's SDK declares. Gated because one person read the groups and the filters were chosen on 15 of the 28 public repositories read: of 50 groups read on the 13 no filter was fitted to, 17 were a value a maintainer would name or already had, 22 a judgment call (a header, a key a protocol defines), and 11 not. Where a constant names the value it was 12, 3, and 5 of 20. No target. A group says the spelling is kept in step by hand in that many files.",
    "mass": "Total complexity mass: sum over functions of CC x sqrt(SLOC). A change reports the mass it grew and the mass it removed apart, each for production and for tests, so a refactor that removes mass reads differently from one that adds it.",
    "mass_8w": "Net mass per week for the last eight weeks of the range, oldest left. Each row scales to its own minimum and maximum, so it shows shape, not sign or size.",
    "mass_grown": "Gross mass the change added: for each function present after the change, max(0, mass_after - mass_before) summed, matched by name within the same file or, for code moved between files, by name, CC, and size. New functions count in full. Production files and test files are summed apart (test_mass_grown). A change that grows mass in many small functions differs from one that grows a single function.",
    "mass_removed": "Gross mass the change removed: for each function present before the change, max(0, mass_before - mass_after) summed, with deleted functions counted in full and moved code not counted. mass_net is mass_grown minus mass_removed. Production and test files are summed apart (test_mass_removed). A refactor should show mass_removed near or above mass_grown.",
    "max_cognitive": "Highest cognitive complexity in any function.",
    "max_len": "Longest function in lines.",
    "max_nesting": "Deepest control nesting in any function.",
    "max_params": "Largest parameter count on any function.",
    "median_depth": "Per week in architecture.periods: the median depth of the week's depth_dirs, and median_depth_attributed the median of their depth_attributed. depth counts a method matched by name as shown and depth_attributed counts it as hidden, so the true median lies between the two, and the text prints them as a range when they differ. Null when the week has no such directory. A median over a handful of directories jumps when one directory crosses the 5-name line, so read it with depth_dirs.",
    "new_case_set": "x/new-case-set, on change with --experimental: a switch, an if chain, or a table the change added that enumerates cases the tree already enumerated somewhere else. It is the pass behind result.case_sets (see case_sets) over the tree at the end of the change: a family is reported when the change added one of its places and at least one other was there before. A place is new when the change added its first line and the changed files hold more places with exactly its cases than they did, so a place that moved or was indented is not, and a place that shares 3 cases with one the file held before under other cases is that place edited and is not new either. The finding sits on the first line of the new place, its match names the file and function of a place that was there, and its note lists up to three of the cases, up to three of the places, and how many edits a new case now needs, which is the family's sites. A change that adds several places to one family reports the first and names the second. --brief prints the note as the reason of the next line. Not reported: a family whose every place the change added, since nothing was there to read from, and whatever case_sets leaves out. Only change reports it, so label cannot find its id. When a hit is right, have the new place read the table or call the function that is there. When the places should stay apart, say why in a slop-strata:ignore comment on the finding's line.",
    "new_duplicate": "x/new-duplicate, on change with --experimental: code the change added that repeats code the tree already holds, or that the change added twice. Each file is read as a sequence of statements, an if or a loop being one statement and each line of its body another. A run of added statements is a repeat when it lines up, statement for statement, with a run elsewhere: the same tokens in the same order once identifiers and literals are set aside. Its score is the tokens of its new statements less 8 for each distinct identifier or literal pair the two sites differ in, which is a parameter a shared function would take. At most 3 such pairs are allowed and a score of 20 is needed. A statement whose shape more than 4 other places in the tree hold counts for less, and for nothing at 32, so an error check or a log line cannot carry a finding. The signature of a function, class, or type lines up and never counts, and a run never ends on an if or a loop whose body is outside it. Two more passes read what the runs left. Stretches too short alone are added up when they sit between the same two functions, in any order and with other statements between them: a copy with a line added, dropped, reshaped, or moved. Their differences count once across all of them, the sum must reach 20, and the note says how many stretches there are. Then each expression of 20 tokens or more in a new statement (a call with its arguments, a condition, a literal, a chain of calls, a type) is looked up among the tree's expressions and scored the same way, so a repeat inside two statements of different shape is found; its note starts 'repeats an expression of' and advises a name. A change that adds one copy several times reports the first, and the note ends with how many times and where. The finding sits on the first line of the new copy, its match names the file and function it repeats, and its note says where that is (this file, the same directory, or another), up to two more places, the size, and what differs. --brief prints the note as the reason of the next line. Not reported: a file in a test directory or under a directory named example, demo, sample, bench, benchmark, e2e, playground, or template (or the plural), under template-\u003cname\u003e, or under __testfixtures__, where a copy is the point; a file of Storybook stories (.stories. in its name); a statement the change also removed somewhere, as written or under other names, which makes a move, a re-indent, or a body pulled into a helper; a run right behind the code it repeats in the same function, which is two arms of one branch; two files of one name, whatever their extensions, that are 80% alike, which is a tree kept in step with a copy of itself; and generated files. An expression is not reported in a signature, inside the function that already holds it, or when it holds statements, as a call does that is passed a function; stretches are not added up inside one function. It does not see an expression under 20 tokens or one taken apart over several statements, stretches that add up to under 20, or a copy that renames more than three things. Only change reports it, so label cannot find its id. When a hit is right, call or widen the code the note names. When the two sites should stay apart, say why in a slop-strata:ignore comment on the finding's line.",
    "new_jsx_shape": "x/new-jsx-shape, on change with --experimental: a block of markup the change added that the tree already held under other text and attribute values. It is the pass behind result.jsx_groups (see jsx_groups) over the tree at the end of the change: a group is reported when the change added one of its sites and at least one other was there before. A block is new when the change added its first line and the changed files hold more blocks of its shape than they did, so a block that moved, was indented again, or had only its text or attribute values edited is not. The finding sits on the first line of the new block, its match names the file and unit of a site that was there, and its note gives the elements of the block, up to three of the sites that were there, and how many values the sites differ in. A change that adds several sites to one group reports the first and names the second, and a new block inside a new block already reported is not reported again. An x/new-duplicate finding whose lines the new block covers is dropped for this one, so a copied block is reported once. --brief prints the note as the reason of the next line. Not reported: a group whose every site the change added, since nothing was there to reuse, and whatever jsx_groups leaves out. Only change reports it, so label cannot find its id. When a hit is right, make the block one component that takes what differs as props and use it at both sites. When the sites should stay apart, say why in a slop-strata:ignore comment on the first line of the new block, written as {/* ... */} after the opening tag, or on the line above it where a // comment can stand.",
    "new_sibling": "x/new-sibling, on change with --experimental: a class, a type, or a module the change added that is written like one the tree already held. It is the pass behind result.sibling_sets (see sibling_sets) over the tree at the end of the change: a set is reported when the change added one of its sites and at least one other was there before. A class or a type is new when the change added the line that declares it, and a module when the change added its file. Either must also be one more of its name than the changed files held before, so one that moved to another file is not new, and it must not be in line with a container the change took away under another name, which is a rename. The finding sits on the first line of the new class or type, or of a new module's first function, its match names the file and the name of a sibling that was there, and its note names up to three of the siblings that were there and up to three of the shared members, and gives how many statements line up and how many differ. One finding per set: a change that adds several siblings to one set reports the first and names the second. --brief prints the note as the reason of the next line. Not reported: a set whose every site the change added, since nothing was there to extend, methods added to a class that was there, and whatever sibling_sets leaves out. The rule does not know of x/new-duplicate, which may report stretches of the same new class beside it. Only change reports it, so label cannot find its id. When a hit is right, extend what is there: a shared base class, a generic, or a table of what differs. When the copies should stay apart, say why in a slop-strata:ignore comment on the finding's line or the line above.",
    "next": "result.next: up to three places to look at, in a fixed order: production functions over CC 10 that grew or were added (largest mass grown first), then new rule hits not in the baseline (by file and line), then a stated intent the numbers argue against (the \"reads as\" clause). A change is worth a look exactly when the list is not empty. change --brief prints the reading and these lines, and nothing when the list is empty. With --exit-code the status is 1 when it would print and 0 when it would not. Look at the first item before you merge or commit. With --json --context, snippets carry the after-side source lines of each next function, up to 80 lines each.",
    "noise_floor": "Median absolute deviation of weekly net mass across the history window. A week whose net sits within one floor of the median week shows no measurable change, so each is marked within_noise and printed with a ~.",
    "oscillating_funcs": "Functions whose body changed in at least 3 separate commits within any 14 days, keyed by file path and function name, counted per directory and overall. oscillating_test_funcs counts functions in test files apart, and the JSON lists the 10 busiest production functions with path, name, line, changes, and span_days. A commit changes a function when a diff hunk adds a line inside it or removes one from between its lines, and the function already existed. Commits of the bot author class do not count. A function rewritten again and again in two weeks means its shape was not settled before the code: decide the interface first, or split the function so the unstable part is small. A formatting commit can supply all three changes, a red-green test loop on one function reads as oscillation though it is deliberate, a function that gains one entry per feature, such as a key map or a command table, counts on every addition, a squash merge hides the changes inside a branch, and a small function has little to change except all of it. A file renamed through git's rename detection keeps its history, and a function renamed or moved to another file starts again. Same-named functions in one file count once per commit, and anonymous functions are not followed.",
    "over_50": "Functions longer than 50 lines. Each is a candidate to split, not a violation.",
    "over_limit": "The share of mass_grown sitting in functions whose cyclomatic complexity exceeds 10, for production files (test_over_limit for tests). A change that concentrates its growth in already-complex functions is the shape erosion measures. Split the function or reduce its branches before adding to it.",
    "p90_cognitive": "90th percentile cognitive complexity (nesting-weighted decision count, approximating the SonarSource definition).",
    "p90_len": "90th percentile function length in lines. Long functions carry context a reader must hold; watch the max, not the mean.",
    "p90_nesting": "90th percentile control-nesting depth. Deep nesting is the shape refactors actually need to fix.",
    "p90_params": "90th percentile parameter count. Many parameters signal a missing abstraction or a config object.",
    "pairing": "Author classes compared on the same week and directory: net mass moved and commits each. A cell is provisional under 5 commits.",
    "precision": "Per rule, tp over tp plus fp from the labels file, with n the number of labels. Under 10 labels the figure is provisional and printed with *. The tier column says whether the rule runs by default or only with --experimental. No rule changes tier from this number: the 80% bar is the owner's call, and the number is evidence for it from one repository.",
    "provisional": "Set on a survival cohort, test class, or directory whose rate rests on too few samples: under 30 lines for a cohort, under 5 test files for a test class, under 5 functions for a directory's erosion (on the smaller side of a change). The rate still prints, marked * in text output.",
    "pure": "Share of young deaths in a hunk that removed lines and added none, against a hunk that replaced them in place. A deleted file counts as pure, and a rewrite whose removal and addition fall in separate hunks reads as pure. High pure with high whole_file and self reads as experiments pruned by whoever ran them. Low pure with high within_7d reads as young code rewritten in place: settle the design before writing the code.",
    "rank": "Per family in result.case_sets: weight times the number of sites, which the families are listed by. A family ranks high when many places enumerate it and its cases are rare in the tree. Per group in result.jsx_groups: elements x (sites - 1) - holes, which the groups are listed by: the elements one shared component per block would take out of the tree, less one for each value it would have to take as a prop. A group ranks high when a large block sits in many places under few differences. Per set in result.sibling_sets: the sum, over the set's shared members, of the tokens of the member's statements that line up at every site holding it times one less than the number of those sites, which the sets are listed by: the tokens one shared copy of each member would take out of the tree. Nothing is charged for what the shared copy would have to take as a parameter, so read differing beside it. A set ranks high when long members line up across many sites. Per group in result.literal_groups: files x uses / written, or files alone when a constant names the value, rounded to two decimals, which the groups are listed by: the files that have to agree on the spelling, weighed by the share of the places where a slip changes what the program does. Where a constant names the value every place counts, since each could read the constant.",
    "readd": "Share of young deaths that came back: a later commit, within 30 days of the death, added a line to the same file with the same trimmed text. deaths_30d carries the counts: readd_at_risk, readded, and readded_by_revert, overall and per directory, and the text table prints readded over readd_at_risk. Only lines of 30 or more bytes match, because shorter ones such as \"return nil\" recur by habit, and readd_at_risk counts only such deaths that had 30 full days of walk after them, the way survival leaves out a line too young to reach its horizon, so a window under 30 days old shows a dash. A line removed and added in one commit is a move or an edit and never counts, and each death and each added line match once. A death in a revert commit, or a line re-added by one, counts in readded_by_revert instead, since undoing a revert is deliberate. A high share means code was cut and then rebuilt in the same place: agree on the design before writing it. A low share beside high pure and whole_file means pruned code stayed gone. It misreads formatting commits that rewrap lines, regenerated fixtures and golden files, a red-green test loop that deletes and restores a line, and squash merges that hide what happened inside a branch. Code that reappears in a file with another name, or in 29 bytes or fewer, is not seen.",
    "removable": "Per group in result.duplicate_groups: tokens x (sites - 1) - 8 x parameters, the tokens a merge of the group into one function would take out, with 8 charged for each argument the function would need. The sum over all groups is duplicate_groups.removable. No statement is counted in two groups.",
    "reverts": "Commits in the range that revert another change.",
    "rework": "Young lines (\u003c=30 days) removed by fix commits: fixes to things just built.",
    "rule_hits": "Slop-rule findings. Each carries the rule id, file, and line; a baseline file holds the ones already accepted. Experimental rules report only with --experimental.",
    "s30_ci": "Greenwood 95% confidence interval for a cohort's survival at 30 days, as [low, high] clipped to 0 and 1. It widens when few lines reach the horizon, so read a wide interval as a weak estimate.",
    "s90_ci": "Greenwood 95% confidence interval for a cohort's survival at 90 days, on the same basis as s30_ci.",
    "s_30d": "Survival at 30 days: the Kaplan-Meier share of lines still alive at 30 days (cohort rows) or of test files not deleted within 30 days (class rows). Null when too few reached 30 days; see survival and test_survival.",
    "s_90d": "Survival at 90 days, on the same basis as s_30d.",
    "self": "Share of young deaths removed by the author who wrote the line, compared by commit author email. High self means people clean up their own work. A high share of other-author removals on agent-born lines (by_class) means cleanup is left to someone else: review agent output before it lands. With one author in the window self is always 1.0, so text output hides the column. A person with two emails reads as two authors, and a rebase that rewrites authors hides the original.",
    "shown": "Per directory on snapshot: the directory's declared names that a production file of another directory uses. A name is a function, type or class, constant, or variable at file scope, or a method (Type.method). Use decides it and visibility does not: export, a capital letter, a leading underscore, and __all__ change nothing. A top-level name is attributed: resolved imports tie the use to the file that declares it, through re-exporting files, so a same-named symbol elsewhere is not counted, and an import that is bound and never used, or a re-export nobody consumes, is not a use. A method cannot be attributed, so it is matched by name alone; see shown_by_name for how many of shown are such guesses. Any other directory is outside, a parent or a subdirectory included, as for fan_in. Test files neither count as users nor have their names counted. A name the world uses and no file of the tree does (a library's public API) reads hidden. Imports the tool cannot resolve (see import_edges) are not uses.",
    "shown_by_name": "Per directory on snapshot (by_name in the table): how many of shown are methods matched by name alone, and shown_mass_by_name how much of shown_mass. There is no type inference, so a method counts as shown when any file of another directory reads a member of that name off a value that is not an imported module, once anything outside imports the directory. A method named like an unrelated member (get, String, value) is counted too: on a hand-checked sample about six in ten were real uses, while every method left hidden was truly unused outside. Read it as how soft shown is: shown minus shown_by_name is certain, and the rest is an upper bound. These names are never listed anywhere.",
    "shown_mass": "Per directory on snapshot: the complexity mass (CC x sqrt(SLOC), see mass) of the functions behind the shown names. A function's mass, with its closures', goes to the declaration that holds it, and a method's to the method. A constant or a type has none.",
    "sibling_sets": "result.sibling_sets on snapshot with --experimental: classes, types, and modules written in parallel, with total and the 20 sets that rank highest. A set says its sites are one thing written out several times, which a shared base class, a generic, or a table of what differs would hold once. What is compared is a container and its members. A container is a class with its methods (kind class), a Go type with the methods one file gives it (kind type), or a file with its top-level functions (kind module), and a member is one of those functions whose body holds 20 tokens or more, so an accessor, a forwarder, and a stub an interface asks for are no member. Python methods named with double underscores are not read. A body is its statements, each by the shape the duplicate pass stores: the statement with identifiers and literals set aside. Two members of one name are in line when the statements they hold in the same order, shape for shape, carry 70% of the tokens of both (twice the shared tokens over the sum). Two containers of one kind and one language are siblings when 2 members or more are in line, those hold 60 tokens or more, and that is a third of the tokens of the smaller container's members. Only pairs that share 2 function names each held by at most 40 containers are compared, so names a framework dictates everywhere (get, run, main) join nothing by themselves. The container whose joins weigh most and every container joined to it are a set, then the same over what is left, and no container is in two sets, so one large family whose members are not all alike can come out as two sets. Per set: kind, sites, members (the names of the shared members: in line between the site the set was gathered around and enough others that half the sites hold it), statements (how many statements of the shared members line up at every site that holds the member), tokens (the tokens of those statements, counted once), differing (the statements of the shared members that do not line up, summed over the sites), and rank. Per site: path, name (the class or type, and for a module the file name without its extension), line and end_line (of the class or type declaration with its methods, and for a module from its first function to its last), and others (the site's own members of 20 tokens or more that the set does not share). Left out because they are parallel on purpose: test files and test directories, the stand-alone directories and story files x/new-duplicate leaves out, directories named scripts, migrations, alembic, seed, seeds, seed_data, seeders, or fixture, generated files, two classes of one name or of one name once async and sync are taken out, two modules of one name in one directory, and two files of one name that are 80% the same statements as written. Not seen: siblings whose members have different names, a container with one member, members under 20 tokens, classes of different languages, and fields, which are not compared at all. It overlaps duplicate_groups on purpose: a group there is one stretch of statements wherever it sits, and a set here is whole containers, so a copied class is one row here and often several groups there, and members built of statements too common to be a group there still line up here. Most sets are not news to that pass: of the 34 sets read, 26 had a duplicate group at two of their sites, but only 10 had one among the 20 groups a snapshot lists, so what this list adds is mostly the grouping by container and the rank. Gated because one person read the sets and the last guard (two files of one name) was chosen on repositories read first: of the 34 sets read among the top 5 of each of twelve public repositories that list any, 23 were sets a maintainer would give a shared base, a generic, or a table, 8 a judgment call, and 3 two flavors of one API or one file per locale that the guards did not catch; of the 19 in the four applications no guard was fitted to, 14, 5, and 0. A set of vendor connectors or of notification handlers that implement one interface with other text looks the same as a copy and is listed too. Sets are few: fourteen of the 22 libraries read list none. No target. A set says a change to the shared members needs an edit per site.",
    "skipped": "Files excluded by config, size, binary content, minified shape, or generated code, each named with its reason. Generated means a generator marker in the first five lines, or linguist-generated in the working tree's root .gitattributes.",
    "split_candidates": "result.split_candidates on snapshot with --experimental: the functions, tests and examples aside, that hold a block worth moving into a function of its own, with total and the 20 a split relieves most. A function is read once its cognitive complexity reaches 15. Every run of statements at one nesting level in it is tried: a whole loop or branch, a loop body, a branch arm, or up to 4 statements of one. For a run, split.cognitive and split.cc are the run's as a function of its own, so the nesting it sat under no longer counts against it, and split.remainder and split.remainder_cc are the function's without it. split.cognitive and split.remainder must each be 5 or more. split.drop is the function's cognitive complexity less the larger of the two, which is what the split takes off the worse of the functions it leaves, and it must be 5 or more and a fifth of the function's complexity. The run with the largest drop is listed, among the 64 largest, every statement of a block before a part of one that drops as much. It holds no defer and no yield. split.exits counts the return, break, and continue statements in the run that leave it, and a run is listed only when each could be a return of the new function with nothing for the caller to check: no break leaves it; its returns are the end of the function, or the run always ends in a return, a raise, or a throw, or each hands back an error from a Go function whose last result is one; its continues are the end of a loop's body. A run is passed over when it is some of a list of like sections and not all of it: it stops next to a branch whose header has the shape of the branch at that end of the run, names and literals aside, or it is every statement of one arm of such a branch. Loops are not read that way. It is also passed over when the statement ahead of it declares one of its inputs and nothing else names that variable: the run that starts with the declaration competes in its place. split.inputs, at most 4, counts the variables declared outside the run that the run reads before it gives them a value. split.outputs, at most 1, counts the ones the run gives a value that is read afterwards: by a later line, by the next pass of a loop around the run, or by the function around a closure. Each name is resolved to the scope that declares it, so a closure's use of a variable of the function around it counts, a method's receiver, self, cls, and this do not, a global or an import is no variable, and a name declared again in a block is another variable. There are no types: a write through a field or an index counts as a read. Two things are still missed: a variable one arm of a branch writes and another arm reads is an input the count can leave out, and so is one a loop body writes on one pass and reads under another branch on a later one. On the top five of twelve public repositories, read by one person, 45 of 60 were a block to move as it stands, 14 a judgment call, and 1 wrong, and the rules were chosen on those twelve. On the top five of ten more, which the rules were frozen before, 20 of 43, 19, and 4, which is 47%. That is under the 80% a list has to clear to be on by default, so it needs the flag. No target: it is a place to start reading a long function, ranked by how much a cut there would take off it.",
    "stale_baseline": "In counts.stale_baseline, on snapshot: baseline entries that no finding of the scored tree carries the id of. The finding was fixed, its line or matched text was edited, or it is an import cycle that shrank, which the entry still covers by its members. Entries of rules this run did not execute (experimental rules without --experimental, ast-grep packs, x/last-ref, x/new-duplicate, x/new-case-set, x/new-jsx-shape, x/new-sibling) are not counted. Run slop-strata snapshot --write-baseline to drop them, so a fixed finding that comes back reads as new. It never changes the exit status. change and history do not score the whole tree against the baseline and always report 0.",
    "support": "Per pair in architecture.hidden_coupling: the sum, over the commits counted in co_changes, of one over the number of other source files the commit changed. A commit of just the two files adds 1 and a sweep over twenty-one files adds a twentieth, so many wide commits weigh less than a few narrow ones. Pairs are ranked by it.",
    "suppressed": "In counts.suppressed, the findings a run set aside: baseline (accepted in the baseline file), ignored (an inline ignore or nolint comment with a reason), and labeled_fp (a false-positive label). A finding counts once, under the first of ignored, labeled_fp, and baseline. A count that grows faster than the hits get fixed means findings are being muted: read the ignore reasons and labels in review.",
    "surface_widened": "result.surface_widened on change: each directory, present before and after, whose count of attributed shown names rose (see shown), with shown_before and shown_after, up to five of the names other directories use now and did not before (newly_shown, with newly_shown_count for all of them), and depth_before and depth_after, which are depth_attributed on each side. Methods matched by name alone never open a row and are never listed, because the match may be another type's method: newly_shown_by_name only counts them, as an unverified upper bound. Both trees are measured whole, so the cause may be a new use in a file of another directory. A rename that swaps one shown name for another is not reported. Information only: it adds nothing to next, and --brief and --exit-code ignore it. When a name you meant to keep internal appears here, the change made another directory depend on it.",
    "survival": "Kaplan-Meier share of a cohort's added lines still alive at 30 and 90 days, per monthly cohort. At-risk is approximated at month granularity. Null when too few lines in the cohort have run as long as the horizon.",
    "table_mass": "Mass of case tables, kept apart from mass and erosion: a map literal of 15+ structured entries scores as a switch with one branch per entry, entries x sqrt(lines). A snapshot's hotspot leaves table_mass out, so a hand-written case table never ranks a directory.",
    "test_survival": "Share of test files added in the range that were deleted within 30 and 90 days, split by the adding commit's author class. Only adds observed inside the range count. Live files younger than a horizon are left out, and the share is null when none is old enough.",
    "top_functions": "On change, up to 10 functions the change grew most, largest mass_grown first, with file, start line, CC and mass before and after, and whether the file is a test file. A new function has cc_before 0. Open these first in review: they hold the growth. On snapshot, the 10 heaviest functions of the tree by mass, with file, start line, CC, SLOC, mass, and test; the text view lists the top 5 production functions. Split the top one before adding to it.",
    "touches_90d": "Commits that touched a directory or file in the 90 days ending at the scored revision's commit date, merges counted once through their first parent. It is the multiplier in a snapshot's hotspot. A change counts the last 90 days from today for its hotspot flag, which marks a directory with 10 or more touches.",
    "uses": "Per group in result.literal_groups: the places, among written, where the code branches or looks something up on the string: a comparison, a membership test, a switch case, a subscript key, or the key of a lookup call. A slip in the spelling there is not an error anywhere: the branch is just never taken. Per site: the uses in that file.",
    "virality": "For each rule: the first commit that hit it, then how hits spread afterward per author class and across directories (spread_dirs). Answers whether a pattern gets copied once it lands.",
    "weight": "Per family in result.case_sets: the sum, over the family's cases, of the natural logarithm of one more than the tree's places per place that names the case, rounded to two decimals. A case few places name weighs much, and one that half the tree's switches name weighs little, so a family of generic words (left, right, error) ranks under one of the program's own constants.",
    "whole_file": "Share of young deaths that died because their file was deleted, against a partial edit to a file that lives on. High whole_file means whole attempts are discarded, which is pruning. A file renamed or split with its lines kept does not count, because moved lines are not deaths. A file deleted and recreated with changes reads as whole_file for the old lines.",
    "widest_surface": "Per directory on snapshot: widest_file is the file with the most shown names and widest_shown is how many. One long list is harder to learn than the same count spread over several short ones, so this names where a wide surface sits.",
    "within_7d": "Share of young deaths within 7 days of the line being written: age_le_1d plus age_2_7d over total. A high share with low pure means code rewritten in place within a week: agree on the shape of the change before writing it. A repository that commits rarely has few deaths in the first day, so read the count with the share.",
    "within_noise": "Set on a week whose net mass lies within one noise_floor of the window's median week.",
    "written": "Per group in result.literal_groups: the places that write the string out, over every file the pass reads, the declarations of constants that name it among them. Per site: the places in that file. See literal_groups for what a place is."
  },
  "repo": "\u003crepo\u003e",
  "result": {
    "base": "HEAD~1",
    "delta": {
      "erosion_after": 0,
      "erosion_before": 0,
      "lines_added": 12,
      "lines_deleted": 5,
      "mass_grown": 5.291502622129181,
      "mass_net": -0.13070247972677507,
      "mass_removed": 5.4222051018559565,
      "over_limit": 0,
      "test_mass_grown": 0,
      "test_mass_net": 0,
      "test_mass_removed": 0,
      "test_over_limit": 0
    },
    "dirs": {
      ".": {
        "erosion_after": 0,
        "erosion_before": 0,
        "hotspot": false,
        "mass_net": -0.13070247972677507,
        "provisional": true
      }
    },
    "end": "HEAD",
    "files": [
